 2. A different Fed view 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios CEO Jim VandeHei highlights what he sees as the most overlooked part of the Fed's Warsh era so far: 

 When Mike and I are curious about the " real economy ," we talk to sources at big-box stores for a read on real-time sales trends or friends at super regional banks to gauge the mood and money movement.

 To us, that's the real world and real data, imperfect but less imperfect than dated Bureau of Labor Statistics surveys built for a different time and economy. That's why we think the most interesting thing to watch long-term at the Fed is the seemingly boring data task force Warsh appointed last month to improve the "quality and timeliness of real economic signals that inform the Federal Reserve's policy judgments."

 The members: Harvard economics professor Raj Chetty , former Walmart CEO Doug McMillon and University of Chicago economics professor emeritus Kevin Murphy . The big picture: AI should surely help aggregate and better analyze more real-time data feeds to help policymakers make better decisions. If not, AI will be a total flop.

 Imagine a parallel Fed that uses more fine-tuned, real-time data feeds, analyzed without economic worldview bias or personal interest of Fed governors. The bottom line: We've known Warsh for years, and his interest in AI and rethinking data is real and intense. We'd be surprised if the ultimate manifestation of his "new chapter" isn't deeply infused with the work of McMillon &amp; Co.

