 4. 📞 Trump on Iran: &quot;We are low-keying it&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo: Eric Lee/Getty Images President Trump signaled yesterday that he's prepared to allow economic pressure on Iran to mount — as opposed to ordering a new military offensive — even as the country continues to defy the U.S.

 This is a massive strategic shift, after threatening Iran repeatedly and then watching them ignore, mock and even bully U.S. negotiators. Iran is now the one making bombastic and unrealistic demands. Only a week ago, Trump was on the verge of ordering a return to major combat operations. But he didn't make any new military threats during a phone call with Axios' Barak Ravid.

 Trump also didn't express any anger or frustration about Iran holding up an announcement on a deal with Oman to reopen the Strait of Hormuz. "We are low-keying it," Trump told Barak. "We are only semi-negotiating with them. We are just watching Iran with its huge inflation and the fact they have no money." Trump stressed that Iran "is in very bad shape" economically and has no money to pay its troops. The U.S. naval blockade has exacerbated the Iranian regime's economic crisis, Trump added.

 "It will work out," Trump said. "It always works out. It's like a chess game." Share this story . 

 
 
