 5. 🤷‍♂️ COVID&#x27;s enduring mystery 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 More than six years after COVID swept the world, there's still no definitive answer about its true origins — and likely never will be, Axios' Caitlin Owens writes. 

 Why it matters: For all of the political vitriol and intelligence disputes over the topic, missing evidence means the debate over whether it jumped from animals to humans or came from a lab may never be satisfyingly settled. The big picture: There have been numerous assessments over the past six years of whether COVID jumped naturally from animals — potentially in a Chinese wet market — or was the product of a leak from a Chinese lab.

 But the conclusions use language like "suggests" or "likely" or "probable," building cases through the biology of the virus, declassified intelligence reports and other circumstantial evidence. Keep reading . 

 
 
